In Java, there is no direct `Tree` collection in the standard library, but you can use classes like `TreeSet` and `TreeMap` from the `java.util` package that internally use a Red-Black tree, which is a type of self-balancing binary search tree.

Here’s a brief overview of these classes and how to use them:

### `TreeSet`
`TreeSet` is a NavigableSet implementation based on a TreeMap. Elements are ordered using their natural ordering, or by a Comparator provided at set creation time.

```java
import java.util.TreeSet;

public class TreeSetExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeSet<Integer> treeSet = new TreeSet<>();

        // Adding elements
        treeSet.add(10);
        treeSet.add(5);
        treeSet.add(20);
        treeSet.add(15);

        // Iterating through the TreeSet
        for (Integer value : treeSet) {
            System.out.println(value);
        }

        // Checking for a specific element
        System.out.println("Contains 10? " + treeSet.contains(10));

        // Removing an element
        treeSet.remove(5);
        System.out.println("After removing 5: " + treeSet);
    }
}
```

### `TreeMap`
`TreeMap` is a NavigableMap implementation based on a Red-Black tree. It orders its keys based on their natural ordering or by a Comparator provided at map creation time.

```java
import java.util.TreeMap;

public class TreeMapExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeMap<String, Integer> treeMap = new TreeMap<>();

        // Adding key-value pairs
        treeMap.put("Apple", 10);
        treeMap.put("Banana", 20);
        treeMap.put("Cherry", 15);
        treeMap.put("Date", 5);

        // Iterating through the TreeMap
        for (String key : treeMap.keySet()) {
            System.out.println(key + ": " + treeMap.get(key));
        }

        // Checking for a specific key
        System.out.println("Contains 'Banana'? " + treeMap.containsKey("Banana"));

        // Removing a key-value pair
        treeMap.remove("Cherry");
        System.out.println("After removing 'Cherry': " + treeMap);
    }
}
```

### Custom Tree Implementation
If you need a more generic tree structure (e.g., binary tree, AVL tree, etc.), you will typically need to implement this yourself or use third-party libraries.

Here’s a simple example of a custom binary tree in Java:

```java
class Node {
    int value;
    Node left, right;

    public Node(int value) {
        this.value = value;
        left = right = null;
    }
}

public class BinaryTree {
    Node root;

    public BinaryTree() {
        root = null;
    }

    void insert(int value) {
        root = insertRec(root, value);
    }

    Node insertRec(Node root, int value) {
        if (root == null) {
            root = new Node(value);
            return root;
        }
        if (value < root.value)
            root.left = insertRec(root.left, value);
        else if (value > root.value)
            root.right = insertRec(root.right, value);

        return root;
    }

    void inorder() {
        inorderRec(root);
    }

    void inorderRec(Node root) {
        if (root != null) {
            inorderRec(root.left);
            System.out.print(root.value + " ");
            inorderRec(root.right);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BinaryTree tree = new BinaryTree();

        tree.insert(50);
        tree.insert(30);
        tree.insert(20);
        tree.insert(40);
        tree.insert(70);
        tree.insert(60);
        tree.insert(80);

        tree.inorder();
    }
}
```

This custom implementation shows how to create a simple binary tree with insertion and inorder traversal functionalities.

