# Start Nginx server
CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]
```

In this Dockerfile:

- We start with the official Nginx base image.
- We remove the default Nginx configuration file because we'll replace it with our custom configuration.
- We copy the custom Nginx configuration file (`nginx.conf`) from the local filesystem to the `/etc/nginx/conf.d/` directory inside the container. This configuration file will define our reverse proxy settings.
- We copy the build files of your React.js project from the local filesystem (assuming they are located in the `frontend/build` directory) to the `/usr/share/nginx/html` directory inside the container. These files will be served as the frontend of your application.
- We expose port 80 to allow external access to the Nginx server.
- We specify the command to start the Nginx server using the `CMD` instruction.

You'll need to create a custom Nginx configuration file (`nginx.conf`) that defines the reverse proxy settings to forward requests to your Node.js Express backend services. Here's a basic example of what `nginx.conf` might look like:

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name localhost;

    location / {
        root /usr/share/nginx/html;
        index index.html index.htm;
    }

    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://backend:3000/;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }
}
```

Replace `http://backend:3000/` with the URL of your Node.js Express backend service. In this example, we assume the backend services are running on port 3000 within the container.

Finally, you'll need to set up your directory structure and place the Dockerfile, nginx.conf, and frontend build files accordingly. Then, build the Docker image using `docker build` command and run the container using `docker run` command.

