Yes, you can definitely centralize the navigation logic by creating a custom hook that wraps `useNavigate`. This way, you can apply consistent logic or error handling in one place. Here’s an example of how to create and use such a hook:

### Custom Navigation Hook

```javascript
// src/hooks/useCustomNavigate.js
import { useNavigate } from "react-router-dom";

const useCustomNavigate = () => {
  const navigate = useNavigate();

  const customNavigate = (path, options = {}) => {
    // Apply any global logic, such as logging or error handling
    console.log(`Navigating to ${path}`);

    // Perform the actual navigation
    navigate(path, options);
  };

  return customNavigate;
};

export default useCustomNavigate;
```

### Usage in Components

Now, in your components, you can replace `useNavigate` with `useCustomNavigate`:

```javascript
import React from "react";
import useCustomNavigate from "../hooks/useCustomNavigate"; // Adjust the path as needed

