The `404` error typically indicates that the requested resource could not be found on the server. In this case, it means that the endpoints you are trying to access from your Vue.js application are not available at the URL you provided.

Here's how to troubleshoot and fix this issue:

### 1. **Check Node.js Server**

Ensure your Node.js server is running and that the endpoints are correctly defined and accessible. You can verify this by using tools like `Postman` or `curl` to test the endpoints directly.

For example, check if `http://localhost:3000/students` returns the expected data.

### 2. **Check API Endpoints**

Confirm that the endpoints in your Vue.js code match the ones defined in your Node.js Express server. Ensure the URL paths and HTTP methods (`GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`) are correct.

For instance, if your Express server is set up to handle endpoints like `/students`, make sure this is exactly how it's spelled and structured in your Vue.js code.

### 3. **Check CORS**

If your Vue.js app is running on a different port than your Node.js server, you need to make sure that your Node.js server is set up to handle Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS).

You can enable CORS in your Express server like this:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const cors = require('cors');
const app = express();

app.use(cors());
app.use(express.json());

// Your routes here...

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server is running on port 3000');
});
```

Install `cors` if you haven’t already:

```sh
npm install cors
```

### 4. **Check URL Paths**

Ensure that the URLs used in the Axios requests match the endpoints on the server. Double-check for typos or incorrect paths.

### 5. **Console Logging**

Add `console.log` statements in your `loadStudents` method to ensure it's being called and check the URL it's trying to access.

Example:

```javascript
methods: {
  loadStudents() {
    console.log('Fetching students from:', 'http://localhost:3000/students');
    axios.get('http://localhost:3000/students').then(response => {
      this.students = response.data;
    }).catch(error => {
      console.error('Error fetching students:', error);
    });
  },
  // Other methods...
}
```

### 6. **Verify Network Requests**

Open your browser’s Developer Tools and go to the Network tab. Check if the requests are being made correctly and see the response or error message.

### 7. **Update Vue.js Code**

Ensure your `StudentComponent.vue` uses the correct URL and methods:

```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <h1>Student List</h1>
    <ul>
      <li v-for="student in students" :key="student.id">
        {{ student.name }} ({{ student.age }})
        <button @click="edit(student)">Edit</button>
        <button @click="deleteStudent(student.id)">Delete</button>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Add New Student</h2>
    <form @submit.prevent="addStudent">
      <input v-model="newStudent.name" placeholder="Name" required>
      <input v-model="newStudent.age" type="number" placeholder="Age" required>
      <button type="submit">Add</button>
    </form>

    <div v-if="editStudent">
      <h2>Edit Student</h2>
      <form @submit.prevent="updateStudent">
        <input v-model="editStudent.name" placeholder="Name" required>
        <input v-model="editStudent.age" type="number" placeholder="Age" required>
        <button type="submit">Update</button>
        <button @click="cancelEdit">Cancel</button>
      </form>
    </div>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
import axios from 'axios';

export default {
  data() {
    return {
      students: [],
      newStudent: { id: 0, name: '', age: 0 },
      editStudent: null,
    };
  },
  methods: {
    loadStudents() {
      axios.get('http://localhost:3000/students')
        .then(response => {
          this.students = response.data;
        })
        .catch(error => {
          console.error('Error fetching students:', error);
        });
    },
    addStudent() {
      axios.post('http://localhost:3000/students', this.newStudent)
        .then(response => {
          this.students.push(response.data);
          this.newStudent = { id: 0, name: '', age: 0 };
        })
        .catch(error => {
          console.error('Error adding student:', error);
        });
    },
    updateStudent() {
      axios.put(`http://localhost:3000/students/${this.editStudent.id}`, this.editStudent)
        .then(() => {
          this.loadStudents();
          this.editStudent = null;
        })
        .catch(error => {
          console.error('Error updating student:', error);
        });
    },
    deleteStudent(id) {
      axios.delete(`http://localhost:3000/students/${id}`)
        .then(() => {
          this.loadStudents();
        })
        .catch(error => {
          console.error('Error deleting student:', error);
        });
    },
    edit(student) {
      this.editStudent = { ...student };
    },
    cancelEdit() {
      this.editStudent = null;
    },
  },
  mounted() {
    this.loadStudents();
  },
};
</script>

