Sure! To handle the arrow keys (up and down) for navigation in the `DataList` component, you can update the `handleKeyDown` function as follows:

```jsx
import React, { useEffect, useState } from "react";
import { format } from "date-fns";
import { enGB } from "date-fns/locale";
import { useDispatch, useSelector } from "react-redux";
import { fetchData } from "../../redux/dataSlice1";

const DataList = ({ itemSelectionHandler = () => {} }) => {
  const dispatch = useDispatch();
  const dataList = useSelector((state) => state.data);
  const [selectedItemIndex, setSelectedItemIndex] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    dispatch(fetchData());
  }, [dispatch]);

  const showData = (id) => {
    itemSelectionHandler(id);
  };

  const handleKeyDown = (e) => {
    const currentDataLength = dataList.length;
    if (currentDataLength === 0) return;

    if (e.key === "ArrowDown") {
      setSelectedItemIndex((prevIndex) =>
        prevIndex < currentDataLength - 1 ? prevIndex + 1 : prevIndex
      );
    } else if (e.key === "ArrowUp") {
      setSelectedItemIndex((prevIndex) => (prevIndex > 0 ? prevIndex - 1 : 0));
    } else if (e.key === "Enter") {
      showData(dataList[selectedItemIndex]._id);
    }
  };

  return (
    <div
      style={{ maxHeight: "400px", overflowY: "auto" }}
      onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}
      tabIndex={0}
    >
      <ul style={{ listStyle: "none", padding: 0 }}>
        {dataList.map((item, index) => (
          <li
            key={item._id}
            onClick={() => showData(item._id)}
            tabIndex={index === selectedItemIndex ? 0 : -1}
            className={
              selectedItemIndex === index ? "selected-item" : "not-selected-item"
            }
            style={{
              fontWeight: selectedItemIndex === index ? "bold" : "normal",
              padding: "15px",
              cursor: "pointer",
            }}
          >
            [ {format(new Date(item.date), "dd/MMM/yyyy", { locale: enGB })} ] -
            : {item.title}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
};

export default DataList;
```

In this updated component, we use the `selectedItemIndex` state to keep track of the currently selected item index. We set the `tabIndex` attribute of each `li` element to `0` for the selected item and `-1` for the rest. This way, only the selected item will be focusable and receive keyboard events. The `handleKeyDown` function now handles the `ArrowDown`, `ArrowUp`, and `Enter` keys.

- When the `ArrowDown` key is pressed, the `selectedItemIndex` is incremented if it's not already at the last item in the list.
- When the `ArrowUp` key is pressed, the `selectedItemIndex` is decremented if it's not already at the first item in the list.
- When the `Enter` key is pressed, the `showData` function is called with the ID of the currently selected item to handle the selection.

The `onKeyDown` and `tabIndex` attributes are set on the container `div` to ensure that it receives keyboard events and is focusable. The styles are also updated to handle the selected and non-selected items with different background colors.

