To consume the GraphQL endpoints in a React application, you can use the `Apollo Client` library, which is a popular choice for working with GraphQL in React. Here's how you can set up and use `Apollo Client` to consume the GraphQL endpoints in your React components:

1. Install Dependencies:
First, install the required dependencies for `Apollo Client` and `React Apollo` in your React project.

```bash
npm install @apollo/client graphql
```

2. Set up Apollo Client:
Create a new file named `apolloClient.js` and configure `Apollo Client` with the URL of your GraphQL server.

```javascript
import { ApolloClient, InMemoryCache } from '@apollo/client';

const client = new ApolloClient({
  uri: 'http://localhost:8080/graphql', // Replace with your GraphQL server URL
  cache: new InMemoryCache(),
});

export default client;
```

3. Wrap the App with Apollo Provider:
In your `index.js` or `App.js`, wrap your React application with `ApolloProvider` to make the client available to all components.

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom';
import { ApolloProvider } from '@apollo/client';
import client from './apolloClient';
import App from './App';

ReactDOM.render(
  <ApolloProvider client={client}>
    <App />
  </ApolloProvider>,
  document.getElementById('root')
);
```

4. Write GraphQL Query:
In your React component, use the `useQuery` hook from `@apollo/client` to fetch data from the GraphQL server.

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import { useQuery, gql } from '@apollo/client';

const GET_ALL_DATA = gql`
  query {
    getAllData {
      id
      date
      title
      htmlText
      tags
      isPrivate
    }
  }
`;

const DataList = () => {
  const { loading, error, data } = useQuery(GET_ALL_DATA);

  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;
  if (error) return <p>Error: {error.message}</p>;

  return (
    <div>
      {data.getAllData.map((item) => (
        <div key={item.id}>
          <h2>{item.title}</h2>
          <p>{item.htmlText}</p>
          <p>{item.date}</p>
          <p>{item.isPrivate ? 'Private' : 'Public'}</p>
          {/* Render other data fields */}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
};

export default DataList;
```

5. Use the Component:
Finally, use the `DataList` component (or other components) in your application to display the data fetched from the GraphQL server.

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import DataList from './DataList';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>GraphQL Data List</h1>
      <DataList />
    </div>
  );
};

export default App;
```

