To create endpoints for saving, updating, and deleting data in the Spring Boot application using GraphQL, we'll need to define corresponding mutations in the GraphQL schema and implement the resolver methods to handle the operations. Here's how you can do it:

1. Update the GraphQL Schema:
In the `schema.graphqls` file, add mutation definitions for saving, updating, and deleting data.

```graphql
type Mutation {
  saveData(date: String!, title: String!, htmlText: String!, tags: String!, isPrivate: Boolean!): Data
  updateData(id: ID!, date: String, title: String, htmlText: String, tags: String, isPrivate: Boolean): Data
  deleteData(id: ID!): Boolean
}
```

2. Implement Mutation Resolvers:

```java
import com.graphql.kickstart.tools.GraphQLMutationResolver;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class DataMutationResolver implements GraphQLMutationResolver {
    private final DataRepository dataRepository;

    public DataMutationResolver(DataRepository dataRepository) {
        this.dataRepository = dataRepository;
    }

    public Data saveData(String date, String title, String htmlText, String tags, boolean isPrivate) {
        Data data = new Data();
        data.setDate(date);
        data.setTitle(title);
        data.setHtmlText(htmlText);
        data.setTags(tags);
        data.setPrivate(isPrivate);
        return dataRepository.save(data);
    }

    public Data updateData(Long id, String date, String title, String htmlText, String tags, boolean isPrivate) {
        Data data = dataRepository.findById(id).orElse(null);
        if (data != null) {
            if (date != null) {
                data.setDate(date);
            }
            if (title != null) {
                data.setTitle(title);
            }
            if (htmlText != null) {
                data.setHtmlText(htmlText);
            }
            if (tags != null) {
                data.setTags(tags);
            }
            data.setPrivate(isPrivate);
            return dataRepository.save(data);
        }
        return null;
    }

    public Boolean deleteData(Long id) {
        dataRepository.deleteById(id);
        return true;
    }
}
```

3. Update the GraphQL Controller:

```java
import graphql.ExecutionInput;
import graphql.ExecutionResult;
import graphql.GraphQL;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
public class GraphQLController {
    private final GraphQL graphQL;

    public GraphQLController(GraphQL graphQL) {
        this.graphQL = graphQL;
    }

    @PostMapping(value = "/graphql", consumes = "application/json", produces = "application/json")
    public ExecutionResult graphQL(@RequestBody String query) {
        ExecutionInput executionInput = ExecutionInput.newExecutionInput()
                .query(query)
                .build();
        return graphQL.execute(executionInput);
    }
}
```

