To read the contents of an Amazon S3 bucket and retrieve the file history using a Spring Boot Java program, you can use the AWS SDK for Java. This example assumes that you have set up your AWS credentials and added the necessary dependencies to your Spring Boot project.

Here's a basic outline of how you can achieve this:

1. Add the AWS SDK dependency to your `pom.xml`:

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.amazonaws</groupId>
    <artifactId>aws-java-sdk-s3</artifactId>
    <version>1.12.86</version> <!-- Use the latest version -->
</dependency>
```

2. Create a Spring Boot service to interact with S3:

```java
import com.amazonaws.services.s3.AmazonS3;
import com.amazonaws.services.s3.AmazonS3ClientBuilder;
import com.amazonaws.services.s3.model.*;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

@Service
public class S3Service {

    private final AmazonS3 s3Client;

    public S3Service() {
        this.s3Client = AmazonS3ClientBuilder.defaultClient();
    }

    public List<S3ObjectSummary> listObjects(String bucketName) {
        ListObjectsV2Request request = new ListObjectsV2Request().withBucketName(bucketName);
        ListObjectsV2Response response = s3Client.listObjectsV2(request);
        return response.getObjectSummaries();
    }

    public List<String> getObjectHistory(String bucketName, String objectKey) {
        List<String> history = new ArrayList<>();
        try {
            VersionListing versionListing = s3Client.listVersions(new ListVersionsRequest().withBucketName(bucketName).withPrefix(objectKey));
            for (S3VersionSummary versionSummary : versionListing.getVersionSummaries()) {
                history.add("Version: " + versionSummary.getVersionId() + ", Last Modified: " + versionSummary.getLastModified());
            }
        } catch (AmazonS3Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
        return history;
    }
}
```

3. Create a Spring Boot controller to expose endpoints:

```java
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PathVariable;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

import java.util.List;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/s3")
public class S3Controller {

    private final S3Service s3Service;

    @Autowired
    public S3Controller(S3Service s3Service) {
        this.s3Service = s3Service;
    }

    @GetMapping("/list/{bucketName}")
    public List<S3ObjectSummary> listObjects(@PathVariable String bucketName) {
        return s3Service.listObjects(bucketName);
    }

    @GetMapping("/history/{bucketName}/{objectKey}")
    public List<String> getObjectHistory(@PathVariable String bucketName, @PathVariable String objectKey) {
        return s3Service.getObjectHistory(bucketName, objectKey);
    }
}
```

In this example, the `S3Service` class provides methods to list objects in an S3 bucket (`listObjects`) and retrieve the object history (`getObjectHistory`). The `S3Controller` exposes REST endpoints to interact with these methods.

